  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 240 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\n(Wednesday - Monday)\n\nThere is still some uncertainty with the exact location of the front \non Wednesday morning, but the most likely location is near the I-\n44/I-55 corridors in Missouri and Illinois respectively. Widespread \nshowers and thunderstorms should continue to percolate during the \nmorning hours along/poleward of this boundary. A gradual decrease in \ncoverage is expected as the low-level jet and associated moisture \nconvergence slowly wanes with time. There will be a pretty sharp \ntemperature difference across the front Wednesday afternoon. North \nof the boundary across parts of northeast Missouri and west-central \nIllinois, low clouds and lingering rain will really hamper \ntemperatures. Highs may not get much higher than the mid 70s to near \n80 degrees. To the southeast on the warm side of the front, \nwidespread mid 90s are forecast though this may be a bit too warm if \noutflow and/or clouds linger. Regardless, dewpoints pooling near the \nfront should result in 100F+ heat index values within the advisory \narea on Wednesday so the heat advisory was extended in time all the \nway through Friday. The chances for outflow or thicker clouds are \nless across that area later this week as the front moves back to the \nnorth as a warm front. There is now pretty high confidence that near-\nrecord highs will continue at least through Saturday. Once the \nboundary does retreat northward, dewpoints should come down each day \nThursday through Saturday. In other words, a bit of a transition to \na \"drier\" heat. Heat index values on Saturday may not even be much, \nif at all, above the ambient air temperature. That is the reason the \nheat advisory was not extended all the way through Saturday. If \ndewpoints are a bit higher than forecast, more widespread ~100F heat \nindex values would occur so it is possible that another day could be \nrequired. \n\nIn the more bad news if you don't like heat department, there has \nbeen a pretty substantial change for late this weekend into early \nnext week. The mid/upper level trough on both the GEFS/EPS has \ntrended more cutoff and slower as it moves across the northern \nPlains/Upper Midwest. That means a shift toward a slower cold \nfrontal passage. Sunday looks most likely to be a continuation of \nthis very late season heat wave with another day topping out in the \n90s for most locations. This more prolonged period of anomalous \nwarmth should equate to one of the warmest first 20 days of \nSeptember at all 3 of our climate stations, and probably THE warmest \nat KSTL. Monday is the day with the most uncertainty now, and high \ntemperatures will depend on the exact timing of the front. If the \nfront is delayed further, you can likely book another day of \nanomalous warmth with highs in the 90s. If the front does make it \nthrough, highs closer to normal (70s/80s) would be expected. \n\n\n(Monday Night - Next Tuesday)\n\nThere is still a strong signal that the cold front will finally make \nit through by Tuesday, though the closed mid/upper level low and \noverall \"blockiness\" of the pattern does shred at least some minor \ndoubts. There still remains differences on the strength of the air \nmass behind the front early/mid next week, with the EPS still quite \na bit cooler than the GEFS. A safe bet is for temperatures to \nfinally get back closer to normal, though whether it ends up a bit \nbelow or slightly above normal is still uncertain.",
